&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Core Features of GitHub Accounts
A GitHub account comes with powerful features that support development.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;4.1 Repositories&lt;br&gt;
A repository (repo) is a storage space for a project.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It includes:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Source code&lt;br&gt;
Documentation&lt;br&gt;
Images or assets&lt;br&gt;
Version history&lt;br&gt;
Repositories can be:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Public (anyone can see)&lt;br&gt;
Private (only selected users can access)&lt;br&gt;
4.2 Commits&lt;br&gt;
A commit is a saved change in code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each commit includes:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What changed&lt;br&gt;
When it changed&lt;br&gt;
Who changed it&lt;br&gt;
Good commit messages help teams understand development history.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;4.3 Branches&lt;br&gt;
Branches allow developers to work on different versions of a project.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Example:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Main branch → stable version&lt;br&gt;
Feature branch → new feature development&lt;br&gt;
This prevents breaking the main project.&lt;/p&gt;

